Problem Understanding
You are given a matrix mat and an integer k.

You must:

Shift even-indexed rows (0, 2, …) → left by k
Shift odd-indexed rows (1, 3, …) → right by k

Then check:

👉 Does the matrix remain exactly the same after these shifts?

⚙️ Core Insight (Most Important)

Instead of actually shifting rows…

🔥 We simulate the final position using math

Because:

Shifting takes time
But computing index positions is O(1)
🔁 How Shifting Works

Assume row length = n

Also:

k = k % n

Because after n shifts → row becomes same again

🔹 Case 1: Even Rows (Left Shift)

Example:

Original: [1 2 3 4]
Left shift by 1 → [2 3 4 1]
Mapping:

Element at index j comes from:

(j + k) % n

But for comparison, we reverse it:

original[j] == original[(j - k + n) % n]
🔹 Case 2: Odd Rows (Right Shift)

Example:

Original: [1 2 3 4]
Right shift by 1 → [4 1 2 3]
Mapping:

Element at index j comes from:

(j - k + n) % n

Comparison:

original[j] == original[(j + k) % n]
🧩 Final Algorithm (Step-by-Step)
✅ Step 1: Get dimensions
m = number of rows
n = number of columns
✅ Step 2: Reduce k
k = k % n
✅ Step 3: Traverse matrix

For every cell (i, j):

🔸 If row is EVEN:
Check:
mat[i][j] == mat[i][(j - k + n) % n]
🔸 If row is ODD:
Check:
mat[i][j] == mat[i][(j + k) % n]
❌ If any mismatch → return False
✅ If all match → return True
🔍 Intuition (Very Important)

Think like this:

“After shifting, will this element still be in the same place?”

Instead of moving elements, we ask:

Where did this element come from?
🧪 Example
mat = [[1,2,1,2],
       [5,5,5,5]]

k = 2
Row 0 (even → left shift)
[1,2,1,2] → shift → [1,2,1,2]

Same ✔️

Row 1 (odd → right shift)
[5,5,5,5] → shift → [5,5,5,5]

Same ✔️

Final Answer:
True
⏱️ Complexity
Type	Value
Time	O(m × n)
Space	O(1)
